#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45abb029-42f5-5297-b793-f893178e0c29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation Implied by the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea31dc28-2d88-5e14-a6ce-271bab6dbb95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404947} {:answer/id #uuid "6039a47e-6f8f-5e29-ba8e-82d0b3500015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404948} {:answer/id #uuid "81a9da62-f570-521a-b138-36cf46985648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404949} {:answer/id #uuid "cca91f0e-0f4b-5db6-b557-f9826f488f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404950} {:answer/id #uuid "22f15cbc-6a53-56fd-b72f-217fe891dc5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6039a47e-6f8f-5e29-ba8e-82d0b3500015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c)=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404948} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c83438b-68cb-5418-93a9-22781afa8c79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2033ad74-5082-5dec-974c-c157b392c0a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-98116" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=1$ and $f(b)=6,$ then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c?$" :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ 6-1 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨2,3⟩-⟨1,2⟩) \\\\ 5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 5 & ={f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2803179-deb8-5b5d-8dc3-c87b7f30ccbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404954} {:answer/id #uuid "00a43c2f-3146-563d-a70e-8ed6a8db819c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404955} {:answer/id #uuid "06c16dd4-4550-535b-882e-e29298a652c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-2-x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404956} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf2d0ae-13a3-53c2-9b69-e902b7337d53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-2+2x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404957} {:answer/id #uuid "311b8139-e8df-52d9-8b84-27e1171d5a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "311b8139-e8df-52d9-8b84-27e1171d5a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404958} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecf12e78-7f08-5ce3-b44f-e89da0101f91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02cd9397-a795-5b68-a5d4-f55d0d92f60a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98553" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+∇f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=-2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & ={e}^{y}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=2, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =x{e}^{y} &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=-1{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =⟨2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+∇\\,f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨2,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ \\\\  & =-2+2(x+1)-2(y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5af3dd4b-8ac7-5c57-a6e6-d30e50a37bbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1+x+y-\\frac{z}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404961} {:answer/id #uuid "be9eb651-901e-5a25-a042-03b339db014b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1-x+y-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404962} {:answer/id #uuid "75095402-2c53-53be-8b08-c572a93a18b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈1-x+y-\\frac{z}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404963} {:answer/id #uuid "de86e77b-b4ee-53ab-8132-4a467415d8e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-3+x-y+\\frac{z}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404964} {:answer/id #uuid "f010ac91-4556-5c1a-a8d5-df7a790c142d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-3+x-y-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5af3dd4b-8ac7-5c57-a6e6-d30e50a37bbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1+x+y-\\frac{z}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404961} :answer-field/id #uuid "444731d2-c3ed-5654-abe5-5731357ba09e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f0a65ec3-c24d-50c1-a1d2-f9666a246122" :question/math-academy-id "q-98554" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1,e)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,1,e)+∇f(1,1,e)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1,z-e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,1,e)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =y\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(1,1,e)=1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =x\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(1,1,e)=1, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =-\\frac{1}{z}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{z}(1,1,e)=-\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,1,e)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,1,e) & =⟨1,1,-\\frac{1}{e}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,1,e)+∇f(1,1,e)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1,z-e⟩ \\\\  & =0+⟨1,1,-\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1,z-e⟩ \\\\  & =0+1(x-1)+1(y-1)-\\frac{1}{e}(z-e) \\\\  & =-1+x+y-\\frac{z}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a619579-d9b6-5bd2-bff5-218f357e46af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈6-x-2y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404968} {:answer/id #uuid "0eab53fc-ca97-5d47-afd9-665094f1824f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈8+x-2y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404969} {:answer/id #uuid "065cdc40-2925-5499-8214-4d592127bcf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈6-x+y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404970} {:answer/id #uuid "d6df1f7f-7f8e-5052-900b-4474987dee6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈8-x+y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404971} {:answer/id #uuid "093ba831-cf40-528f-8c4d-e63bda2f510c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈6+x+2y-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a619579-d9b6-5bd2-bff5-218f357e46af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈6-x-2y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404968} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4796cde-152c-5b9c-b5c5-10a80fc71912" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec12843c-221e-5977-accb-d9e9aa80cb79" :question/math-academy-id "q-98555" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{9}{x+2y-z}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,1,1)+∇f(2,1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(2,1,1)=3.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =-\\frac{9}{(x+2y-z)^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(2,1,1)=-\\frac{9}{(2+2(1)-1)^{2}}=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-\\frac{18}{(x+2y-z)^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(2,1,1)=-\\frac{18}{(2+2(1)-1)^{2}}=-2, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =\\frac{9}{(x+2y-z)^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(2,1,1)=\\frac{9}{(2+2(1)-1)^{2}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(2,1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1,1) & =⟨{f}_{x}(2,1,1),{f}_{y}(2,1,1),{f}_{z}(2,1,1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,1,1)+∇f(2,1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =3+⟨-1,-2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =3-1(x-2)-2(y-1)+1(z-1) \\\\  & =6-x-2y+z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae8b541b-6c2c-5c34-8f52-8d8bc5c341df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404975} {:answer/id #uuid "83b7d222-6b21-5f46-baad-0be2919bb086" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404976} {:answer/id #uuid "42221465-d5d3-5322-81e1-4397847a1a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=-7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404977} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d814eb-c7d7-579f-9607-8d549d7bda4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=-6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404978} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3e5eca-a564-5d66-b064-7f655185e885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42221465-d5d3-5322-81e1-4397847a1a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=-7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404977} :answer-field/id #uuid "20839a78-520c-5f1d-8f7a-a6481c46623d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a08e4af-7f63-5439-b490-91f36e22b19e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99032" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+21x+23.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+21x+23) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+24x+21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+24x+21 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+7 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+7) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\,-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-1$ and $x=-7.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-1$ and $x=-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd281f17-99d6-5be7-a5a7-e5d777ed4a88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404982} {:answer/id #uuid "80c4a5f6-a26f-5884-b87b-97b1c3d00708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404983} {:answer/id #uuid "1972cef3-5e06-5006-a5e9-98532f424f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404984} {:answer/id #uuid "82ae3c82-b9bb-5ca5-96e5-fbbe4ef1e350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404985} {:answer/id #uuid "1df8665d-93b7-5a6b-9a93-0bb764b11fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82ae3c82-b9bb-5ca5-96e5-fbbe4ef1e350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404985} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a891f36-9832-5e16-a500-7251af8a1737" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb13e83e-9cd0-53cb-aaf0-21f542a77f1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99050" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function  $f(x)={e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & ={(12x-{x}^{3})}^{′}{e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(4-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (2-x)(2+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e62997e2-1f0c-5d4b-9eb1-4b41bb6ab374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404989} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf010c4-f01a-50cf-8d58-06c4c1650590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404990} {:answer/id #uuid "01c6ef37-e7d0-563b-82d0-0dd75adf8406" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404991} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca2e0a4-dadf-59ca-94f1-7367e00a0944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404992} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9a5af9-9a13-510f-84ca-0b15cfce2ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaf010c4-f01a-50cf-8d58-06c4c1650590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404990} :answer-field/id #uuid "6da6d1b2-a92b-5381-b98b-6a6c794e9358" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42dd53a1-4ccb-5086-adb5-70b77ded4e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-99053" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+6}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(2x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(2x+1)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+12-4{x}^{2}-2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-2x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12-2x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 12-2x-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-3$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3099f3c2-846b-5ae3-b13d-62bf2853c8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404996} {:answer/id #uuid "80bf0b4b-0794-5476-96ae-6da113dacb97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404997} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2185e2-fc3d-5abf-9cc2-33c57fd4ee19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404998} {:answer/id #uuid "4278a543-dfc0-5d1f-b8c7-13dc3aeb961d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404999} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea262a0-90f2-5ce8-828c-598aa9e08bf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ea262a0-90f2-5ce8-828c-598aa9e08bf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405000} :answer-field/id #uuid "25281d74-3b09-5c83-b4d4-d24247da42a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "63c773de-f51b-54ba-8cf5-768e0048eb88" :question/math-academy-id "q-99055" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-5)-(x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-5)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)-(x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5+6x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+5}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+5}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=5.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065b8702-9c1f-5408-a110-0b6b1f931f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405003} {:answer/id #uuid "7e04b74f-d9c6-59e7-a8ee-004d2123563f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405004} {:answer/id #uuid "625ac297-0116-50dc-b32e-593a5c25fd5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405005} {:answer/id #uuid "2f10324a-c1cb-5f86-99bd-f736b0ed8050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405006} {:answer/id #uuid "acea3269-c2cc-5d39-87be-8ac3b32540bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e04b74f-d9c6-59e7-a8ee-004d2123563f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405004} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcf1d3ea-9362-58c4-b795-b8796b5019b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "70a98919-dd4e-535e-b918-4ce91d1560b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-99056" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4+6x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "beb5fa66-9dc2-50aa-beaa-310da7fc4c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interval of Convergence of a Given Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f06d73f-7ac7-568b-9656-314a94895833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,,\\,\\frac{2}{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405010} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1a8387-fe3e-5916-b4a6-ea4d748af438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-1,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405011} {:answer/id #uuid "7adb9997-41ff-5cb0-979f-150b16d9d414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-1,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405012} {:answer/id #uuid "f964925b-5620-5342-a2ca-f54010365538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,,\\,\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405013} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3dd73d-0351-5ca8-8016-a7615446f186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed1a8387-fe3e-5916-b4a6-ea4d748af438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-1,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405011} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc050dae-f829-533e-a886-22db5daf03b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbe3ba98-4d51-53d7-a612-90e7db738fe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-9926" :question/problem "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}=\\frac{x}{{1}^{3/2}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3/2}}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{3/2}|x| \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{3/2} \\\\  & =|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $|x|<1.$ So, the radius of convergence is $1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-1,1).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-1$ and $x=1$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{1}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the $p$-series test.  So the endpoint $x=1$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{{1}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=-1$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-1,1].$"}]}
